course: dictionary
lesson: 13827
title: Itemise: one thing at a time
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To itemise is to make a list of things one by one, often with the price or amount of each. The word item means a single thing, so itemise means to list each item on its own.
explain: «The shop gave Mum a bill that itemised every single thing she bought.» Each thing has its own line, with its cost next to it. Look at the words around itemised: bill, every single thing, cost. These clues tell you it means listed separately.
explain: «Please itemise the equipment you need for the science project.» Here you write each piece of equipment on a separate line, such as goggles, a beaker and a torch. Ask yourself: would the list still make sense if I put "make a list of each thing" in place of itemise? If yes, you have the meaning.
fact: Itemise is a verb, so you can itemise, it itemises, and it was itemised.
fact: Itemise comes from item, which means a single thing.
fact: An itemised bill or receipt shows each thing you bought and its cost.
fact: In American English, the spelling is itemize.
fact: At school, you can itemise the things you need for a project.
exercise: «The bill itemised every drink and snack we bought.» What does itemised mean? | Hidden under the table | Paid for everything at once with no list | *Listed each thing separately | Thrown away
exercise: «Please itemise the list of things you need.» Which small word is hidden inside itemise? | *item | need | please | list
exercise: Mum asks you to itemise her shopping. What should you write? | Only the total, with no details | *Each item on its own line, with its price | One long sentence about the shop | A drawing of the shop door
test:
«The teacher asked us to itemise our equipment.» What is the teacher asking us to do? | *Make a list of each thing separately | Sing a song about our equipment | Give our equipment away | Tidy the classroom quietly
An itemised receipt shows what? | The name of the shop only | A picture of the till | *Each thing bought and its cost | The weather on the day of shopping
exam:
Which is the best meaning of itemise? | Hide things in a box | Count the clouds in the sky | *Make a list of things one by one, often with prices | Argue loudly about a bill
